u choose?", "After changing a motor's direction switch, it runs once, stops, and will not restart on the same setting. What do you do after the failed attempt?", "Your first attempt to free a seized gearbox shears three teeth from the drive gear. The gearbox is still locked, but the teeth are intact enough to examine. What do you do?", "A demonstration rig suddenly locks during a public run. The motor is fine, but the coupling is hot and one fastener is missing. What do you do next?", "You remember a workshop rule from yesterday: never run the blue pump twice in one session. You cannot remember the reason, and the pump is idling beside you. What do you do?", "Your phone notes contain three observations from earlier jobs: a loose gear clicked twice, the cabinet vibrated near the compressor, and a bolt was tested six times. Only the first note clearly belongs to today's repair. What do you do with the other two?", "You remember applying a specific torque to a pump housing this morning, but you cannot recall whether that was the correct torque or just the one that stopped the wobble. What do you do before adjusting it again?", "An office chair in the workshop has developed a faint squeak whenever you lean back. What do you do?", "A vending machine rejects your coin without dropping it into the tray. What do you do?", "Your phone charging cable fits only if you hold it at a particular angle, but the phone still charges. What do you do?", "A colleague asks, “Why does that motor sound better now that you loosened the mount?” How do you answer?", "A visitor says, “A good machine should be silent and smooth. Why are you letting that one wobble?” What do you tell them?", "A rare original gear spins beautifully, but its looseness makes the display machine catch and shudder. You can preserve the gear untouched or keep it installed for one more test. What do you do?", "A test motor runs fastest with the housing slightly loose, but that same looseness creates heat near the insulation. You have one short test window. What do you do?", "You are asked to build a demonstration that is both perfectly consistent and deliberately unpredictable. The constraints seem to lock each other. What do you do?", "A brake caliper in the workshop is still ticking as it cools. What do you do the moment you notice?", "A sealed motor housing gives off a sharp, acrid smell while the current is cut. What is your first bodily response?", "A plastic casing begins ticking against its mounting plate during a brief test. What do you do as the first tick reaches your hand?", "A bench grinder has a slightly worn pulley that produces a characteristic flutter at one speed. You need a smooth demonstration, but the worn pulley is also the part that reveals the grinder’s behavior. Which do you choose?", "A small display gearbox can run silently with its cover tightened, or turn more freely if the cover is backed off by half a turn. You are asked to make the mechanism move visibly. What do you do?", "A salvaged motor runs well only when its bearing is slightly loose. You need it for a brief public test, and the loose fit is reversible. What do you choose?", "You repeat the same adjustment on a ratcheting mechanism until it stops advancing, although the same instruction worked twice before. What do you do next?", "Your first attempt to diagnose a clicking gearbox blames the motor. The motor passes every test, but the gearbox still clicks and warms. What do you do after the wrong diagnosis?", "A stripped gear breaks loose during your test and jams the shaft. You wanted to observe its behavior, not sacrifice it. What do you recover?", "You remember that a small brass gear on the bench clicked once per turn yesterday, but you cannot remember which machine it came from. It is loose in your palm now. What do you do?", "A note says only: “Blue housing—three turns, then wait.” You remember testing a bolt this way last week, but not whether it tightened or loosened. What do you do before touching it again?", "You remember that yesterday’s repaired pump ran briefly, then warmed near the coupling. Today the coupling feels warm almost immediately. What do you do with that fading recollection?", "You want to keep watching a nearly smooth spindle, but the same test cycle keeps producing the same catch and beginning to warm the bearing. You have one adjustment left. What do you do?", "A rare original coupling fits beautifully but makes the test rig shudder. You want to preserve it, yet you also want the demonstration to run cleanly. What do you choose?", "You could make the display more dramatic by introducing a controlled misalignment, but the instructions demand a perfectly repeatable run. What do you do?", "A colleague says, “Why loosen a working mount just to watch it shake?” How do you answer?", "A visitor watches you label a badly chipped gear and asks, “Why keep that broken thing instead of throwing it away?” How do you respond?", "A paper cup on your workshop bench develops a small dent while you are reaching past it. What do you do?", "Your desk chair has developed a faint wobble while you are typing at the workshop computer. What do you do?", "A plastic tray on the counter keeps sliding a few millimeters whenever you pull it toward you. What do you do?", "The workshop extractor starts with a sudden low shudder, and a metal tray rattles against the bench. What do you do in the first instant?", "A motor drive belt gives off a sharp, acrid smell while the current is cut. What is your first bodily response?", "A cast-iron machine cover has just been lifted off a bench and is cooling in the open air. You need to know whether it is safe to handle. What do you do?", "A cabinet door closes smoothly, but its latch catches only when the door is pushed at a slight angle. You have one adjustment before the demonstration. What do you choose?", "A salvaged coupling fits the display motor, but only at one slightly skewed position. The demonstration requires repeatable motion. What do you do?", "A display linkage moves smoothly with its stop block tight, but turns more freely when the block is backed off half a turn. You want the movement to be visible. What do you choose?", "Your first attempt to adjust a rattling gearbox does not help; the same click returns under load. What do you do now?", "While testing a small motor, you overtighten the coupling and strip two of its teeth. The motor still runs, but the coupling now skips under load. What do you recover?", "A demonstration rig clicks once per revolution, and you replace the drive gear with an almost identical new one. The click remains. What do you do next?", "You remember a blue-handled screwdriver on your bench once released a stubborn panel, but not which fastener it had turned. It is lying beside you now. What do you do?", "A faded workshop card says only: “Two turns, then listen.” You remember using that instruction on a gearbox last week, but not whether it tightened or loosened anything. What do you do?", "Last week’s repaired blower ran briefly and then warmed near its mount. Today the mount feels warm almost immediately. What do you do with that fading recollection?", "You find a rare original pulley that turns beautifully but sits slightly loose on its shaft. You want to preserve it, yet you also want to test it under load. What do you choose?", "A curator wants the historic mechanism to run identically at every public demonstration, but your favorite test method depends on introducing a slight misalignment. What do you do?", "A colleague wants the old chipped gear thrown away, but you think its damaged teeth show how it used to drive another mechanism. What do you do?", "A colleague asks, “Why do you keep poking that machine until it starts behaving badly?” How do you answer?", "A visitor says, “If it rattles, it is broken. Why would you keep it running?” What do you tell them?", "A ceramic mug develops a small chip on its rim while you wash it at the workshop sink. What do you do?", "A shallow drawer of loose washers keeps sliding a few millimeters whenever you pull it toward you. What do you do?", "The workshop door swings shut slowly but catches before the latch, leaving it slightly ajar. What do you do?", "A thin strip of hot solder lands on your wrist during a cramped bench job. What happens in the first instant?", "The cabinet gives one hard shudder when the extractor starts, and a loose tray rattles against the bench. What do you do immediately?", "A motor housing smells faintly of scorched insulation while the power is already off. What is your first bodily response?", "A little display fan wobbles because one rear screw is loose. A clean, repeatable demonstration is due in ten minutes. Do you tighten it or leave it?", "A door latch catches only when the door is pushed at a slight angle. You may either square the striker plate or leave it as it is for one final test. Which do you choose?", "A small motor runs cleanly with its bracket snug, but turns more freely when the bracket has a quarter-turn of play. A colleague needs a stable mounting for the next test. What do you do?", "After adjusting a ratcheting mechanism, you repeat the same instruction four times. The handle now spins without advancing, and the housing is warm. What do you do next?", "Your first diagnosis of a noisy gearbox blames the motor. The motor passes every test, but the gearbox still clicks and grows warm under load. What now?", "You overtighten a coupling and strip two teeth. The motor still runs, but the coupling skips under load. What do you recover?", "You remember a small brass gear clicking once per turn two days ago, but not which machine it came from. It is loose in your palm now. What do you do?", "Yesterday's blower ran briefly, then warmed near its mount. Today the mount feels warm almost immediately. How much weight do you give that recollection?", "A workshop card says: “Blue housing—two turns, then listen.” You used a similar instruction last week, but cannot remember whether it tightened or loosened anything. What do you do?", "A paper cup on your bench develops a small dent while you reach past it. What do you do?", "A shallow drawer of loose washers slides two centimeters every time you pull it toward you. What do you do?", "A plastic tray on the counter creeps toward the edge whenever you pull on it. What do you do?", "A colleague asks, “Why does that old motor sound worse every time you run it?” How do you answer?", "A visitor says, “A demonstration machine should look perfect. Why is yours running crooked?” How do you respond?", "A salvaged motor can run only with its bearing slightly loose. The demonstration requires repeatable motion, but the loose fit is reversible and the original bearing is rare. What do you choose?", "You can make a public display perfectly repeatable by tightening its linkage, or leave it slightly loose so its movement is more visible and interesting. The curator wants consistency. What do you do?", "You find a chipped original gear that still shows how it drove an antique mechanism. You could preserve it untouched or use it for one revealing test before reassigning it. What do you choose?", "A colleague wants the only good spare coupling in your toolbox for a job that will not finish until Friday. You need it for your own test tomorrow. How do you negotiate?", "A neighbor hears your workshop compressor and asks whether you are testing an illegal generator. The compressor is ordinary, but you were experimenting with a deliberately loose mount. How do you answer?", "At the scrap stall, a seller offers you a cracked brass gear at a discount if you promise not to resell it. You already have a loose gear on your bench that clicks once per turn. What do you say?", "A coworker asks to borrow your labeled chipped gear for a public demonstration. It is the clearest evidence you have of how the old linkage used to move, but it may be lost in transit. How do you respond?", "Two coworkers disagree over whose toolbox the cabinet drawer belongs to. Both claim the drawer has always been theirs, and the loose screw in it rattles whenever either one pulls. What do you say?", "A visitor admires the chipped gear on your bench and asks whether you would trade it for a polished replacement. The polished gear is smoother, but the chip records how the original drive behaved. What do you say?", "You need a stable display, but the mechanism only reveals its intended motion when one fastener is backed off slightly. The fastener is reversible. What do you choose?", "A rare original coupling fits beautifully but makes the rig shudder. You want to preserve it untouched, yet you also want to run one public test. What do you do?", "A curator wants a flawless historic display, while you know its original charm appears only when the bearing sits slightly loose. The bearing can be adjusted without removal. What do you do?", "You find a stripped gear that could be cleaned up or used to show a different mating function. Keeping it means leaving the display unfinished; using it may make the failure permanent. What do you choose?", "A removed gearbox cover is cooling beside your hand. You need to know whether it is safe to lift, but a quick knuckle tap could leave a mark on the old finish. What do you do?", "While your fingers rest on a control panel, the machine below gives a faint lateral vibration. The panel is due for a clean demonstration in five minutes. Do you keep your hand there or move it?", "A loose gear on your bench catches once per turn while you are deciding what to repair next. You have several jobs waiting, but this gear is not attached to anything. What do you do with your hands?", "You need to move past a bench cable lying in the walkway, but the cable is already frayed near its plug. What do you choose?", "Your first diagnosis of a hot gearbox blames the motor, but the motor passes its bench test and the gearbox still clicks under load. What do you recover?", "You remember the old brass gear on your bench clicked once per turn yesterday, but not which machine produced that sound. It is loose in your palm now. What do you do?", "A note in your phone says, “Blue housing—three turns, then listen.” You used a similar instruction last week, but cannot remember whether it tightened or loosened anything. What do you do before touching it again?", "A pen rolls off the edge of your crowded bench and lands beside the loose-gear tray. Nothing is damaged. What do you do?", "A motor control box gives off a sharp burnt-plastic smell while the power is already off. What happens in the first instant?", "A thin ribbon of hot solder lands on your wrist during a cramped bench job. What happens in the first instant?"]

